    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.1.05-13",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Slow growth or decline can create different problems. A shrinking working-age group may make it harder to staff services or support fixed infrastructure costs, depending on productivity and migration. Some facilities may be underused while care demand rises. Training, appropriate technology, service reorganisation and opportunities for participation can respond to different constraints. No single population policy settles every economic effect.",
      "claim_ids": [
